Unreleased
            - Quality
              * The Rust toolchain is pinned in rust-toolchain.toml, so a local
                build and CI use the same compiler version and cannot drift
                apart.
              * Clippy runs with `-D warnings`, so a new lint fails the build
                instead of passing quietly until the next toolchain bump.
            - Performance
              * The grid is stored as one flat row-major allocation instead of
                a vector of rows, so reading a cell is a single index.
              * Walking a cell's neighbours no longer allocates: it fills a
                fixed-size array, which is what every generation algorithm
                does at every step.
              * Verifying and solving a maze index a flat table by cell rather
                than hashing coordinates, which is about nine times faster.
              * The `check` command counts dead ends directly instead of
                building the list to take its length.
            - Fixed
              * `--width 0` and `--height 0` are rejected instead of printing
                an empty maze and exiting 0, so a script can tell that no maze
                was produced.
              * A maze bigger than 25 million cells is refused before anything
                is allocated, with the cell count and the limit in the message.
                `--width 100000 --height 100000` used to run until memory ran
                out; it now fails immediately.
              * The banner, progress and summary lines go to stderr, so stdout
                carries only the maze and the `check` verdicts and can be
                redirected to a file.
              * The library no longer prints. The progress line moved to the
                command line tool, which leaves `Generator` silent for anyone
                using it as a crate.
v0.0.1 - 2026-11-18
            - First release.
            - Features
              * Maze Generation with Backtracking Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Prim's Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Kruskal's Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Aldous-Broder Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Wilson's Algorithm.
              * Maze Generation with Eller's Algorithm.
              * In-memory maze structure with a lossless, versioned text file
                format, so a saved maze loads back exactly as it was written.
              * A library and a command line binary split apart, so the maze
                can be used as a crate as well as from the shell.
              * `generate`, `check` and `solve` commands.
              * Solver that draws the route from the top-left corner to the
                bottom-right corner onto the maze.
              * Reproducible generation through `--seed`, with the seed used
                always reported.
              * SVG export for printing and sharing.
              * Braided mazes (`--braid`) and entrance/exit openings
                (`--entrance-exit`).
            - Quality
              * Unit, integration and property-based tests; the property tests
                check every algorithm at random sizes including the degenerate
                1x1, 1xN and Nx1 shapes.
              * Criterion benchmarks for generation, the variants, the map hot
                paths and file I/O.
              * Continuous integration runs format, clippy, build and test.
              * Docker image published for `nettrash/maze`.
